29,1,,action,Shunichi,,Drainage callouts and August heat readings; Timed counts of weekday crossings,"{""action"":""investigate"",""cards"":[""card_08"",""card_07""],""parse"":""ok""}"
30,2,,status,May,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":1,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
31,2,,status,Minato,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":2,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
32,2,,status,Shizue,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":3,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
33,2,,status,Shunichi,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":4,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
34,2,,status,Victoria,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
35,2,1,talk,Minato,,I found out that only two local firms are willing to do the big digging. They are worried about the clay soil and things slipping.,"{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,May,,"Well, the numbers are in, and they're scary. Planted areas cost three times more to maintain than hard surfaces. We cannot afford a ""green paradise"" that we can't pay for.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Shizue,,I am a bit saddened to report that our previous plantings on the eastern avenue failed because the watering was forgotten. The quality is very inconsistent.,"{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Victoria,,My findings are concerning. Our internal staff has shrunk from eighteen to seven. We are relying heavily on bought-in services for the basics.,"{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Shunichi,,"It's as I feared. The drainage is failing, and the heat is trapping. Plus, people are cutting across the park to get to the tram, ignoring the paths.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Minato,,"@May, if we use too many hard surfaces, will that make the clay soil problem worse? I don't want the park to sink.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,May,,"@Minato, I'd rather it sink a little than have a budget that disappears into a hole every year. We need a plan that is lean and sustainable.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Shizue,,"@Victoria, if we have so few staff, we must choose a plan that is simple. I saw that the civic hall apron is already crumbling. We cannot afford fancy things.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Shizue, exactly. If we only have seven people, we cannot manage a complex garden. We need a plan that utilizes the existing volunteer groups or requires very little.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@May, hard surfaces are great until they turn the park into an oven. The heat readings are already too high. We need a design that handles water and heat, not just a concrete slab.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Minato,,"I think we should be careful about the ""big"" plans. If only two firms can do the work and they are worried about the soil, a simple plan is safer.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,May,,"@Shunichi, I hear you on the heat, but look at the ledger! We are paying a fortune for turf and water. A ""natural"" look that requires constant care is a lie.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Shizue,,"I agree with @May on the cost, but not on the concrete. We need materials that last. The basin from eight years ago is still strong; that is what we should emulate.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Victoria,,"I am worried that any plan requiring ""specialist care"" will fail. We have no one left to do it. We need a plan that fits a seven-person team.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Shunichi,,"We are talking about budgets and staff, but the people are already walking their own paths to the tram. If we build a wall or a fancy hedge there, they'll just kill it.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Minato,,"I just want to make sure we don't start something we can't finish. If the soil is bad, maybe we should avoid deep excavations.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,May,,"Let's be honest: the city is broke. We need a plan that looks decent but costs almost nothing to keep. No fancy water features, no high-maintenance lawns.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Shizue,,"I believe we should look for the plan that uses the most durable, simple materials. Let us not be fooled by a pretty picture of a garden that will die in two years.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Victoria,,"I will support any plan that doesn't assume we have a full staff. If it requires a dozen gardeners, it is a fantasy.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Shunichi,,"Then we need a plan that accepts the tram-commuter flow, fixes the drainage issues, and doesn't rely on a ghost crew of gardeners.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,3,,status,May,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":1,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
56,3,,status,Minato,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":2,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
57,3,,status,Shizue,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":3,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
58,3,,status,Shunichi,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":4,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
59,3,,status,Victoria,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
60,3,1,talk,Minato,,Plan B seems the safest for the soil. It's mostly flat and doesn't need deep digging like the ponds in Plan D or the facilities in Plan C.,"{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,May,,Plan B is the only one that makes financial sense. Paved surfaces are cheap to maintain. Plan A and D are just expensive gardens waiting to die.,"{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Shizue,,"I am very wary of Plan A and D. As I saw with the eastern avenue, we cannot keep plants alive. Plan B is simple, but Plan C's courts might be more durable.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Victoria,,Plan B is the most realistic for our staff. A plaza doesn't need a gardener; it needs a sweeper. Plan A and D would be a disaster for a seven-person team.,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Shunichi,,"Plan B is a heat trap. A paved square in August will be unbearable. However, Plan D is a fantasy given our drainage failures—it'll just be a swamp.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Minato,,"@Shunichi, you're right about the heat. But Plan C has changing facilities, which means deep pipes and digging. The local firms were worried about the clay.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,May,,"@Shunichi, we can put umbrellas in a plaza. We can't put a million dollars of maintenance into a ""wetland"" that turns into a mosquito pit because we have no staff.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Shizue,,"@Minato, you make a good point about the digging. Plan B is the most modest. It doesn't promise a paradise it cannot deliver. It is honest.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Victoria,,I agree. Plan B requires the least amount of daily human intervention. We can't manage a woodland or a wetland with the current rota.,"{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Shunichi,,"Plan B is the ""safe"" choice, but it ignores the tram commuters. They'll just walk across the plaza, which is fine, but it doesn't solve the heat or the water.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Minato,,I think Plan B is the only one that won't get stuck in the mud for three years. I'm worried that any other plan will just lead to delays.,"{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,May,,"It's the only one that won't bankrupt us in three years. I'm voting for B. It's clean, it's simple, and it's affordable.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Shizue,,"I have seen too many ""green"" projects fail here. I would rather have a plain plaza that stays a plaza than a forest that becomes a graveyard of dead saplings.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Victoria,,My priority is the people. We have no one to maintain a forest or a wetland. Plan B is the only one that doesn't set the staff up for failure.,"{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Shunichi,,"I'm not thrilled about the heat, but I'm more thrilled about not having a swamp. Plan B is the only one that doesn't fight the reality of our staffing and budget.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Minato,,I feel better knowing we are all thinking about the same problems. I'll vote for Plan B because it's the most realistic for the builders.,"{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,May,,"Finally, some common sense. Let's pick the one that doesn't cost a fortune to sweep.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Shizue,,"It is a modest choice, but modesty is often the most sustainable path. I am happy to agree with you all.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Victoria,,I can support Plan B. It is the only option that respects the limits of our current human resources.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Shunichi,,"It's a boring choice, but it's the only one that won't be a disaster in two years. I'll go with B.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
